    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As an example of computing the power of random-effects ANOVA designs for estimating variance components, consider the standard half-sib design for estimating additive genetic variance (Chapter 18). Assuming a balanced design, N sires are each mated to n unique and unrelated females. The linear model (Equation 18.1) is $ y_{ij} = \\mu + s_i + e_{ij} $, where $ s \\sim (0, \\sigma_s^2) $ and $ e \\sim (0, \\sigma_e^2) $. Assume further that s and e are normally distributed. Then, from Equation A5.28, the sire effect sum of squares, $ SS_s $, follows a $ \\chi^2 $ distribution, with",
